Basic example and validation case: united-atom polyethylene chain (40 atoms, types 1 = CH2,
2 = CH3), five LAMMPS data files (atom_style full). Each frame changes the bond topology of
the previous frame by one scenario:

  timestep  file                 change vs. previous frame                    bonds
  0         pe_chain.0.data      intact chain 1-2-...-40                        39
  1000      pe_chain.1000.data   scission 8-9 and 21-22                         37
  2000      pe_chain.2000.data   formation 8-22 (recombination of chain ends)   38
  3000      pe_chain.3000.data   flip 30-31 -> 31-15 (broken + formed)          38
  4000      pe_chain.4000.data   flip 33-34 -> 34-12 plus scission 36-37        37

The numbers in the file names are intentional: the natural sort must put timestep 0 first.
Graph test data only: branch points keep the CH2 type and the geometry is not updated.
Regenerate with:  python3 gen.py .

Expected number of reactions per timestep:

  rxn_bond_cutoff   1000   2000   3000   4000
  0                 2      1      1      2
  1, 2              2      1      1      1

At 1000 the two scissions are 13 bonds apart and stay independent for any small cutoff.
At 4000 the flip and the scission have separate cores (atoms 12/33/34 and 36/37, 2 bonds
apart); for cutoff >= 1 their environments overlap and are merged into one reaction.

Usage:
  from atomigraph import AtomiGraph
  ag = AtomiGraph(infile="examples/01_PE_chain_basic/pe_chain.*.data", informat="lammps_data",
                  rxn_bond_cutoff=1)
  ag.read()
  ag.find_reactions()
  print(ag.rxns[["timestep", "edges_before", "edges_after"]])
